  "text": "We are given:\n\n- **4 representatives** to choose from.\n- **5 departments** available: Sales, Marketing, HR, IT, and Finance.\n- **Each representative can only belong to one department.**\n- **At least 2 representatives must come from different departments.**\n\nWe are to find how many **different combinations** are possible.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Total number of ways to assign 4 representatives to 5 departments\n\nThis is a **permutation** problem since the order in which representatives are chosen does matter (i.e., a group of 4 representatives is a combination, not a permutation).\n\nSo, the total number of ways is:\n\n$$\n\\binom{5}{4} = 5\n$$\n\nBut this is only **one way** to choose the 4 departments. The actual combinations are:\n\n- Select 4 departments out of 5: $\\binom{5}{4} = 5$\n- For each of these 5 combinations, assign 4 representatives from the 5 departments.\n\nSo, total number of **combinations** is:\n\n$$\n5 \\times 4! = 5 \\times 24 = 120\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Subtract the cases where all 4 representatives come from the **same department**\n\nWe must ensure that **at least 2** representatives are from **different departments**.\n\nSo, we subtract the number of cases where **all 4 representatives** come from **the same department**.\n\nHow many such cases are there?\n\n- Only one department (Sales, for example) can have 4 representatives: $\\binom{1}{4} = 0$\n\nSo, there are **no** such combinations.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{200}\n$$\n\n**Answer: (B) 200**",
